A||||5||mshill||V6 top end in questionable condition...||||11||
Z||000000||mshill||09-28-2000||09:21 PM||||I just started taking apart my V6 top end to seal up all the oil leaks.  This is an 86GT with about 96K miles on it and it has not been driven for several years (other than putting it in and taking it off the trailer).<P>When I got the valve covers off, the top end was caked (well, it seemed like a coating up to 1/8 inch deep) of what looked liek crusty ashes.  I figured the oil was coked onto the valve train components.  <P>How can I clean this stuff off without contminating all of the oil passages?  I really didn't want to overhaul this engine now cause it did run smooth and had decent compression on at least the back three cylinders when I bought it.<P>I am tempted to remove and replace the pushrods and rocker arms since I have it taken down tis far.<P>Any words of wisdom out there?||||reg||11
Z||000001||80's BOY||09-28-2000||10:19 PM||||i took 5 gallon buckets and had them filled with VARSOL cleaner (much like turpentine) and soaked the upper and lower plenum and intake. after that i washed them out at the hight pressure car wash and it worked out great.||||reg||3
Z||000002||mshill||09-29-2000||05:51 AM||||80's Boy,  thanks for the tip.  I am cleaning the valve covers and plenum as well.  I was looking more for the tops of the heads, without removing them, and the valve train assembly.  I could of course, take the heads off and soak them as well, but if I do that I will want to get some machine work done and then I might as well tear it all apart.  Don't want to do that yet because I will eventually put a 3.4L pushrod in.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||09-29-2000||08:07 PM||||Have no fear when doing your cleaning, as all the guck will go into the oil pan and will not get into the oil passages. When your finished cleaning and scraping, rinse well (very well) pull the pan and clean it out and re-install it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RedOktober||09-29-2000||08:29 PM||||I've used a Shop Vac and a couple of different sized scrapers. Just use some rags and stuff them into the oil drain holes and clean away. Just dont push them in too far cuz you will have a b of a time getting them back out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The excessive coking on the vave covers could meen that there may be other problems, so keep your eyes open. <P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||09-29-2000||10:17 PM||||Going back to the odd cures subject(saw dust in the rear end etc.). Run kerosene in the crank case for a few min.||||reg||1
